UID:feminist-gaming-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Feminist Gaming Fellowship: Open Call 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Feminist Gaming Project is opening applications for its fir
 st Fellowship cohort: five fellows from different creative and research fi
 elds spending three months developing 3D assets\, tools\, and documentatio
 n for its Open Access Asset Library (OAL)\, a free\, open repository of in
 clusive 3D content created by\, with\, and for intersectionally feminist a
 nd diverse communities. Where mainstream marketplaces default to hyper-ath
 letic\, idealized\, or hyper-sexualized characters\, the OAL offers averag
 e bodies\, real postures\, everyday objects\, and the kinds of lives most 
 games leave out (pregnancy and postpartum\, care work\, ageing\, disabilit
 y\, domestic labor). This first cohort is dedicated to 3D content. Support
 ed by the Creative Industries Fund NL and working across the Netherlands\,
  Türkiye\, Germany\, and the UK\, the project challenges exclusionary nor
 ms in how games are made. The fellowship offers a EUR 1\,000 honorarium pe
 r fellow over three months (half at start\, half on completion)\; mentorsh
 ip from the core team and advisors in feminist design\, game studies\, 3D 
 production\, and digital culture\; a peer community across NL\, TR\, DE\, 
 and the UK with biweekly meetings\; visibility through a travelling exhibi
 tion program\, publications\, and public events\; and authorship credited 
 in the OAL under its license terms. Five roles are recruited (one fellow e
 ach): 3D Character Artist for inclusive base meshes\; 3D Character Artist 
 for pregnancy and postpartum assets\; 3D Animator for neutral and everyday
  movement\; 3D Modeller for domestic and care-labor environments\; and Res
 earcher/Writer for documentation and the representation framework\; applic
 ants may rank up to two. Open to 3D artists\, animators\, modellers\, desi
 gners\, researchers\, and writers committed to inclusive and intersectiona
 l representation in games\; prior game-industry experience is not required
 \, and emerging creators\, underrepresented practitioners\, and researcher
 s at the intersection of games\, gender\, and culture are especially encou
 raged\, particularly those based in or connected to NL\, TR\, DE\, or the 
 UK. To apply\, email feministgamingproject@gmail.com with a short statemen
 t (max ~500 words)\, a portfolio or work samples (a link is fine)\, and co
 nfirmation of availability for the three-month period\; name the role(s) i
 n the subject line or statement. The project explicitly asks applicants to
  write statements in their own voice rather than using AI-generated text. 
 Indicative timeline: applications close 12 July 2026\; selection and notif
 ication late July\; fellowship period August to October 2026\; showcase an
 d publication November to December 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Feminist Gaming 
 Project\nLocation: Remote across the Netherlands\, Türkiye\, Germany\, an
 d the UK\nAward: EUR 1\,000 honorarium per fellow (paid half at the start\
 , half on completion)\nDuration: Three months\, August to October 2026\; m
 inimum ~6 hours per week (~75 hours total)\n\nMore: https://feministgaming
 .com/fellowship/index.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://feministgaming.com/fellowship/index.html?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote across the Netherlands\, Türkiye\, Germany\, and the UK
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:center-for-fiction-emerging-writer-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Center for Fiction / Susan Kamil Emerging Writer Fell
 owship
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Fiction / Susan Kamil Emerging Writer Fellowship
  annually supports nine early-career\, New York City-based fiction writers
  at a pivotal point in their careers. Each fellow receives a 5\,000 USD gr
 ant\, dedicated space and time to write in the Writers Studio\, personaliz
 ed editorial mentorship\, opportunities to meet literary agents\, and comm
 unity programming. Applicants must be current residents of one of the five
  boroughs and must remain in New York City for the entire fellowship year.
  An emerging writer is defined as someone who has not yet had a novel or s
 hort story collection published by a major or independent publisher and wh
 o is not currently under contract for a work of fiction\; students enrolle
 d in a degree-granting program during the fellowship year are not eligible
 . The 2027 fellowship application window opens July 1\, 2026 and closes Ju
 ly 31\, 2026.\n\nOrganisation: The Center for Fiction\nLocation: New York 
 City\, USA\nAward: USD 5\,000 grant plus writers studio space\, mentorship
 \, agent introductions\, and community programming\nDuration: One year (th
 e fellowship year)\n\nMore: https://centerforfiction.org/grants-awards/nyc
 -emerging-writers-fellowship/apply-to-the-nyc-emerging-writer-fellowship/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://centerforfiction.org/grants-awards/nyc-emerging-writers-fellows
 hip/apply-to-the-nyc-emerging-writer-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:upe-foundation-curatorial-fellowship-camden-art-centre-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
SUMMARY:Deadline: Upe Foundation Curatorial Fellowship at Camden Art Centre
  2026
DESCRIPTION:Upe Foundation is launching the Upe Foundation Curatorial Fello
 wship at Camden Art Centre as part of its Fall 2026 Curatorial Fellowship 
 Programme. The opportunity invites Baltic early-career curators to apply f
 or a full-time curatorial role at Camden Art Centre\, an institution inter
 nationally acclaimed for its rigorous exhibitions\, residencies\, events a
 nd learning programmes. The fellowship is a full-time\, 12-month position 
 starting Autumn 2026\, with the possibility of a further 6-month extension
  (18 months total) upon review and mutual decision\; relocation to London 
 is required. ROLE: embedded within Camden Art Centre's Exhibitions Departm
 ent\, the Fellow works as Assistant Curator\, supporting the development\,
  coordination and delivery of the exhibitions programme and related projec
 ts and events\, with practical experience across exhibition research\, pro
 duction\, interpretation\, publications\, public programmes\, loans\, inst
 allation and documentation\, and liaising with artists\, galleries\, lende
 rs\, partner organisations\, writers and designers. The Fellow also shares
  research connected to Baltic and broader Central Eastern European artisti
 c contexts. ELIGIBILITY: a demonstrated connection to or sustained engagem
 ent with the Baltic region (Lithuania\, Latvia\, Estonia\, or their diaspo
 ras)\, which may include lived experience\, heritage\, working knowledge o
 f a Baltic language\, relevant research\, or curatorial work with Baltic a
 rtists/institutions/contexts\; early-career curatorial experience (freelan
 ce\, self-organised\, project-based or institutional)\; and a higher educa
 tion qualification (BA\, MA or equivalent) in curating\, art history\, vis
 ual culture or related disciplines. Desirable: working knowledge of Lithua
 nian\, Latvian or Estonian\; fluency in a second language\; experience wit
 h Articheck and with CRM systems (particularly Salesforce). Applicants mus
 t hold the right to work in the UK (via the under-26 'new entrant' Skilled
  Worker route\, the Creative Worker visa route\, or existing right to work
  such as British citizenship or settled/pre-settled status). OFFER: GBP 30
 \,960 salary\, structured professional development within the Exhibitions 
 team\, and opportunities to build professional networks in London and inte
 rnationally. Applications reviewed jointly by Upe Foundation and Camden Ar
 t Centre\, with first-round online interviews and a second interview for f
 inalists. The organizers strongly encourage applicants to write materials 
 in their own words and avoid generic or AI-generated responses. TIMELINE: 
 application deadline 12:00 noon UK time\, Monday 13 July 2026\; interviews
  6 August 2026\; final decision mid-August 2026\; start Autumn 2026. Quest
 ions: recruitment@camdenartcentre.org.\n\nOrganisation: Upe Foundation (in
  partnership with Camden Art Centre)\nLocation: London\, United Kingdom. F
 ull-time\, in-person\; relocation to London is required. Applicants must h
 old the right to work in the UK.\nAward: Salary of GBP 30\,960 per year. A
  full-time Assistant Curator role on a 12-month fixed-term contract\, with
  the possibility of a further 6-month extension (18 months total) subject 
 to formal review and mutual agreement.\nDuration: 12-month fixed-term posi
 tion starting Autumn 2026\, with possible 6-month extension (18 months tot
 al).\n\nMore: https://upefoundation.org/news/open-call-upe-foundation-cura
 torial-fellowship-at-camden-art-centre?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://upefoundation.org/news/open-call-upe-foundation-curatorial-fell
 owship-at-camden-art-centre?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:London\, United Kingdom. Full-time\, in-person\; relocation to Lon
 don is required. Applicants must hold the right to work in the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:justice-joy-summer-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260709
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260710
SUMMARY:Deadline: Justice + Joy Summer Research Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Justice + Joy Research Fellowship is a seven-week virtual s
 ummer program for girls\, young women\, and gender-expansive young people 
 of color (ages 18-26) in the United States who want to explore issues impa
 cting their lives\, communities\, and futures. Fellows identify a topic th
 ey care about\, such as mental health\, education\, reproductive justice\,
  immigration\, AI and technology\, gender-based violence\, beauty standard
 s\, young parenthood\, or another issue affecting their communities\, and 
 develop an original research project grounded in their experiences\, quest
 ions\, and ideas. Throughout the summer\, fellows participate in weekly vi
 rtual sessions\, engage with guest speakers\, build community with peers f
 rom across the country\, and receive mentorship and support as they develo
 p their research. The fellowship culminates in a capstone showcase where f
 ellows present their work to the Justice + Joy community\, including organ
 izational leaders\, partners\, and supporters. No prior research experienc
 e is required\; the program seeks young people who are curious\, committed
  to learning\, and interested in using research as a tool for understandin
 g\, advocacy\, and change. COMMITMENT: weekly virtual sessions on Wednesda
 ys 5:00-6:30 PM ET (9 July - 20 August 2026)\; approximately 2-4 hours of 
 independent work per week\; required orientation on Wednesday 9 July\; dev
 elopment of an individual research proposal or conceptual framework\; pres
 entation at the capstone showcase (tentatively in person\, mid-September 2
 026\, with all travel and lodging covered). WHAT YOU GET: a USD 2\,000 sti
 pend paid upon successful completion\; skills-based training in research m
 ethods\, social justice frameworks\, and community advocacy\; guest speake
 r sessions\; a peer community\; and a real audience for the research (the 
 Justice + Joy board and funders). Apply via the Google Form. Note: an expl
 icit application deadline is not stated\; the required orientation and pro
 gram start is 9 July 2026\, so apply before then.\n\nOrganisation: Justice
  + Joy\nLocation: United States\; fully virtual program. Capstone showcase
  tentatively in person (mid-September 2026)\, with travel and lodging cove
 red.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend paid upon successful program completion\, 
 plus skills-based training\, guest speaker sessions\, and peer community. 
 All travel and lodging for the in-person capstone are covered.\nDuration: 
 Seven-week virtual program\, 9 July to 20 August 2026\, with a capstone sh
 owcase mid-September 2026. Weekly Wednesday sessions 5:00-6:30 PM ET plus 
 approximately 2-4 hours of independent work per week.\n\nMore: https://doc
 s.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfXN_oqSA7G7RRPjhLp-_McFix70m4g8Q_yny0aiBDb-
 qfHvw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfXN_oqSA7G7RRPjhLp-_McFix70m4
 g8Q_yny0aiBDb-qfHvw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; fully virtual program. Capstone showcase tentative
 ly in person (mid-September 2026)\, with travel and lodging covered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:genesis-mission-ai-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Genesis Mission AI Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Horizon Institute for Public Service\, Renaissance Philanth
 ropy\, SeedAI\, and Fulcrum Science are partnering to launch a fellowship 
 that embeds technical AI talent across the US Department of Energy nationa
 l labs to advance the goals of the Genesis Mission\, DOE's flagship scienc
 e and technology initiative launched in late 2025. The Genesis Mission ide
 ntifies 26 national science and technology challenges critical to US leade
 rship and envisions a national platform connecting supercomputers\, experi
 mental facilities\, AI systems\, and unique datasets\, with DOE's 17 natio
 nal labs playing a key role. Genesis Mission AI Fellows are forward deploy
 ed to DOE national labs to bring frontier AI expertise to high-priority sc
 ientific challenges. As core members of the technical staff on a Genesis M
 ission project\, fellows work alongside national lab scientists and partne
 rs and apply AI to national priorities\, from building cutting-edge models
  of plasma dynamics in pursuit of controlled fusion to advancing state-of-
 the-art models for foundational materials discovery. Initial national lab 
 partners include Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhaven National Laborator
 y\, and the Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory\; additional partners may 
 be announced later. COMPENSATION/DURATION: 9-12 months starting fall 2026\
 , with a prorated total annual compensation of USD 200\,000\; fellows are 
 expected to travel to their national lab site at least once a quarter but 
 may work on-site if they prefer. ELIGIBILITY: competitive applicants shoul
 d have at least 2 years of industry experience building AI models or agent
 s (those with industry experience building AI applications or data infrast
 ructure will also be considered)\; scientific domain expertise is not requ
 ired\, but any placement will involve some training and study in a specifi
 c scientific discipline or energy topic\, supported by expert colleagues a
 t the host institution. All fellows must be eligible to work in the United
  States for the duration of the placement\; US employment visas cannot be 
 sponsored\, but US permanent residents (green card holders) are eligible. 
 APPLICATION: initial form requires a CV or resume (max 2 pages) and short-
 answer questions on motivations and experiences (max 200 words)\; successf
 ul applicants complete interviews through partner organizations and nation
 al labs\, and selected fellows are matched to partner labs by scientific d
 omain and availability. The review uses evidence-based best practices incl
 uding blind grading of application materials. TIMELINE: application deadli
 ne 31 July 2026\; final fellow selection August-September 2026\; expected 
 start fall 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Horizon Institute for Public Service (wi
 th Renaissance Philanthropy\, SeedAI\, and Fulcrum Science)\nLocation: Uni
 ted States. Fellows are forward deployed to US Department of Energy nation
 al labs (initial partners: Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhaven National
  Laboratory\, Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory). Remote or in-person\; 
 on-site travel at least once a quarter. Must be eligible to work in the US
  (no visa sponsorship\; US green card holders eligible).\nAward: Prorated 
 total annual compensation of USD 200\,000.\nDuration: 9-12 months\, starti
 ng fall 2026.\n\nMore: https://horizonpublicservice.org/announcing-the-gen
 esis-mission-ai-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://horizonpublicservice.org/announcing-the-genesis-mission-ai-fell
 owship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. Fellows are forward deployed to US Department of En
 ergy national labs (initial partners: Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhav
 en National Laboratory\, Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory). Remote or i
 n-person\; on-site travel at least once a quarter. Must be eligible to wor
 k in the US (no visa sponsorship\; US green card holders eligible).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddc-trygfonden-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Digital Democracy Centre x TrygFonden Fellowship Program 
 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Digital Democracy Centre (DDC) at the University of Souther
 n Denmark\, with support from TrygFonden\, is calling for applications for
  its 2027 fellowship cohort. The program fosters collaboration between res
 earchers\, journalists\, and policymakers exploring the impact of digital 
 technology and AI on media\, politics\, and democracy. Fellows collaborate
  with a self-selected DDC mentor to generate new knowledge\, contribute to
  public debate\, develop networks\, and produce tangible outreach outputs 
 such as policy briefs\, educational packages\, and talks. THREE FELLOWSHIP
  TYPES: (1) academic fellows researching digital technologies and democrac
 y\; (2) journalist fellows covering digital technology and democracy\; (3)
  policy fellows contributing to the regulatory debate. Target group: resea
 rchers\, journalism graduates\, and policymakers in research/educational i
 nstitutions\, media organizations\, ministries\, NGOs\, etc. PRIORITIES: p
 rojects with clear relevance to Denmark or Danish society (including compa
 rative/international projects yielding insights relevant to a Danish conte
 xt)\; in 2027\, projects related to DDC's Hybrid Warfare Initiative (socie
 tal resilience to new forms of international conflict\, with a focus on di
 gital technologies and AI) are also prioritized. EXPECTATIONS: applicants 
 must secure confirmation of mentorship from their desired DDC mentor BEFOR
 E applying (applications without a confirmed mentor are automatically reje
 cted\; PhD students and research assistants are generally not eligible as 
 mentors\, and Sten Schaumburg-Muller is unavailable as a mentor for the 20
 27 call)\; applicants must also coordinate support from their current supe
 rvisors/managers. Fellows engage in DDC activities\, seminars/workshops\, 
 and public outreach\, and submit a short report within two months of finis
 hing. APPLICATION (in English): preliminary project title\, fellowship typ
 e\, brief project description (max 1000 chars)\, detailed description and 
 timeline (max 2400 chars\, including duration\, confirmed mentor\, and rem
 ote/in-office work plan)\, description of activities and collaboration wit
 h external practice partners (max 1000 chars)\, expected product/impact/ou
 tput (max 500 chars)\, a CV (max 5 pages)\, and an overview of the posts f
 or which financial support is sought. Submit the application form (PDF or 
 Word) plus CV as PDFs to ddc@sdu.dk with subject line 'DDC / TrygFonden Fe
 llowship application'. Deadline 1 September 2026\; decisions communicated 
 no later than 1 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Digital Democracy Centre (U
 niversity of Southern Denmark)\, with support from TrygFonden\nLocation: O
 dense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of in-offic
 e stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned to the Dan
 ish working calendar.\nAward: Either a salary subsidy or a stipend to help
  with travel and/or housing\; specific benefits (financial support\, acces
 s to DDC research resources\, flexible working spaces\, speaker series\, n
 etworks) vary by agreement between the fellow\, DDC\, and partnering organ
 izations. Applicants describe the posts they seek support for (flights\, h
 ousing\, research costs\, salary compensation\, stipend\, etc.).\nDuration
 : Flexible\; typically 2-6 months (minimum two months\, can include shorte
 r DDC stays and remote periods)\, longer in exceptional cases. Fellowships
  start anywhere in 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forsknin
 gsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracycentre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowshi
 p-call-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forskningsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracy
 centre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowship-call-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Odense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of
  in-office stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned t
 o the Danish working calendar.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:frame-fellowship-2-ai-safety-content-creators-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260710
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260711
SUMMARY:Deadline: Frame Fellowship 2.0 (Residential Accelerator for AI Safe
 ty Content Creators) 2026
DESCRIPTION:Frame Fellowship is a 10-week residential accelerator in San Fr
 ancisco for content creators educating the public on AI safety. Frame's pr
 emise is that content creators can solve a long-standing bottleneck of exp
 anding reach and trust beyond the AI safety and effective altruism (EA) bu
 bble\; creators today hack attention\, empower mass action and shape cultu
 re. Past Frame Fellows include award-winning filmmakers\, journalists from
  leading newsrooms and ex-activists who transitioned to educating\, empowe
 ring and preparing their communities for the impacts of AGI. Frame helps a
 mplify their impact to millions of people\, connects them to funding and s
 ponsorship opportunities at organizations such as FLI\, CAIS and Seismic\,
  and helps establish their path as a leading content creator. This cohort 
 runs 10 weeks with a stronger mentor and creator network\, access to produ
 ction-ready studios in San Francisco\, housing\, food and a stipend\, plus
  connections to funding and partnership opportunities after the program. T
 WO TRACKS: (1) Independent Track\, build your own channel and become a def
 ining voice on a topic\; (2) Amplifier Track\, embed with an AI org/compan
 y and grow their reach. SUPPORT: Frame covers accommodation\, meals\, flig
 hts and a USD 10\,000 stipend for the 10 weeks so fellows can go all in\; 
 top performers may be eligible for additional grant opportunities beyond t
 he program. Early deadline: 10 July 2026. Apply at https://framefellowship
 .com/.\n\nOrganisation: Frame Fellowship\nLocation: San Francisco\, USA\; 
 residential (in person).\nAward: USD 10\,000 stipend for the 10 weeks\, pl
 us accommodation\, meals and flights covered. Access to production-ready s
 tudios in San Francisco\, a mentor and creator network\, and connections t
 o funding\, sponsorship and partnership opportunities at organizations suc
 h as FLI\, CAIS and Seismic. Top performers may be eligible for additional
  grant opportunities beyond the program. No application fee.\nDuration: 10
 -week residential program\n\nMore: https://framefellowship.com/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://framefellowship.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
LOCATION:San Francisco\, USA\; residential (in person).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-ai-accountability-fellowships-2026-2027@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: AI Accountability Fellowships 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:The AI Accountability Fellowships support journalists working o
 n in-depth AI accountability stories that examine how governments and corp
 orations use predictive\, generative and surveillance technologies to guid
 e decisions in policing\, medicine\, social welfare\, criminal justice\, h
 iring and more. Designed for reporters from all beats\, desks and formats\
 , the fellowship asks applicants to propose a concrete reporting project (
 with evidence of pre-reporting) that uses approaches such as data analysis
 \, records requests and shoe-leather reporting to examine the real-world i
 mpact of algorithms. For the first time this year\, the fellowship also in
 cludes funding\, mentorship and training to develop and execute an impact/
 engagement plan to reach strategic audiences. SUPPORT: up to USD 25\,000 p
 er fellow (up to USD 20\,000 reporting plus USD 5\,000 engagement)\, paid 
 in three installments\; freelancers may budget up to one third as a stipen
 d\, while newsrooms cover staff salaries. Fellows also receive mentors\, s
 pecialized training\, pro bono legal and public records support\, and a la
 sting community of peers. ELIGIBILITY: staff or freelance journalists acro
 ss print\, radio\, video and multimedia\, able to work collaboratively\; r
 eporters may be based anywhere (fellowship is remote). Experience reportin
 g on AI is not required\, but a track record of in-depth\, impactful repor
 ting and investigative/data/explanatory experience is valued. Small teams 
 may apply with a designated lead Fellow. REQUIREMENTS: a mandatory monthly
  1.5-2 hour meeting\, at least one community call\, engagement with other 
 fellows\, and sharing of methodologies and lessons learned\; fellowship co
 mmunication\, meetings and training are in English\, but fellows may publi
 sh in any language. APPLY: submit a 500-word statement of purpose\, a 500-
 word project description with reporting plan\, an engagement plan\, a budg
 et\, three links to recent work\, a letter of commitment from a publishing
  media organization (or editor support letter for staff)\, three reference
 s and a CV. Funded with support from the MacArthur Foundation\, Luminate\,
  Omidyar Network and others. Contact: reacheditorial@pulitzercenter.org. D
 eadline: 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EDT.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoc
 ation: Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is r
 emote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing a
  broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.\nAward: Up to USD 25\,000 per 
 fellow\, paid in three installments (up to USD 20\,000 for reporting and U
 SD 5\,000 for engagement activities)\; covers records requests\, travel\, 
 data analysis and engagement costs. Freelancers may budget up to one third
  of the total as a stipend\; newsrooms are expected to pay staff salaries.
  No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months\, expected September 2026 to Jul
 y 2027\n\nMore: https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunitie
 s-journalists/ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunities-journalists
 /ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is 
 remote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing 
 a broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:harvard-radcliffe-institute-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Harvard Radcliffe Institute: Fellowship Program 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The Harvard Radcliffe Institute Fellowship offers scientists\, 
 writers\, scholars\, public intellectuals\, and artists a year to pursue a
 mbitious projects in a vibrant interdisciplinary setting amid the resource
 s of Harvard University. The Institute welcomes innovative work that confr
 onts pressing social\, scientific\, and policy issues and seeks to engage 
 audiences beyond academia. Reflecting Radcliffe's history\, it welcomes (b
 ut does not limit eligibility to) proposals focused on women\, gender\, an
 d society\, or that draw on the Schlesinger Library's collections. It also
  invites proposals relevant to the Institute's 2024-2029 focus area\, acad
 emic freedom and connecting across difference (intellectual virtues\, free
  and open inquiry\, diversity of thought\, political polarization\, peace 
 and conflict\, inequality\, religious pluralism\, and related higher-educa
 tion policy issues)\, including work that constructively challenges discip
 linary orthodoxies or advances transformative perspectives. SUPPORT: USD 7
 8\,000 stipend plus USD 5\,000 for project expenses\, with relocation\, ho
 using\, and childcare funds available\; fellows must reside in the Cambrid
 ge/Boston area from September through May. ELIGIBILITY: open to individual
 s across career stages\; tenure is not required and applicants need not be
  academics. This is NOT a postdoctoral fellowship\; those currently enroll
 ed in a degree program are ineligible\, as are former Harvard Radcliffe fe
 llows (1999-present). Applicants must meet discipline-specific criteria. A
 PPLY: register on the online portal and select an area (Humanities and Soc
 ial Sciences\; Creative Arts\; Nonfiction and Journalism\; or Science\, En
 gineering\, and Mathematics). Materials: application form\, CV\, 1\,400-wo
 rd project proposal (with bibliography when appropriate)\, a writing or wo
 rk sample\, and three references. DEADLINES: humanities\, social sciences\
 , creative arts\, and nonfiction and journalism by 10 September 2026\, 5pm
  ET\; science\, engineering\, and mathematics by 1 October 2026\, 5pm ET.\
 n\nOrganisation: Harvard Radcliffe Institute\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massac
 husetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and reside in the Cambridge/Bosto
 n area for the full fellowship (September through May).\nAward: USD 78\,00
 0 stipend plus an additional USD 5\,000 for project expenses. Relocation\,
  housing\, and childcare funds may also be provided. No application fee.\n
 Duration: One academic year\, September 2027 through May 2028 (residential
 )\n\nMore: https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/applicat
 ion-information?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/application-info
 rmation?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and res
 ide in the Cambridge/Boston area for the full fellowship (September throug
 h May).
END:VEVENT
