BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:govai-uk-winter-fellowship-2027-research-track@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI UK Winter Fellowship 2027 - Research Track
DESCRIPTION:GovAI's UK Winter Fellowship (Research Track) is a three-month 
 in-person fellowship in London for researchers and policy professionals se
 eking to enter or advance careers in AI governance and policy. Fellows con
 duct independent research on a topic of their choice\, paired with a GovAI
  supervisor from the team or network\; the first two weeks are spent explo
 ring the AI governance landscape before a research proposal is finalised. 
 Research outputs may include a report\, white paper\, journal article\, op
 -ed\, or blog post targeted at an audience relevant to AI governance. Alon
 gside the project\, fellows attend expert Q&As\, weekly seminars\, skills 
 workshops\, work-in-progress meetings\, and social events\, and are encour
 aged to discuss follow-on career opportunities with GovAI's team and netwo
 rk. GovAI alumni have gone on to roles in US\, UK\, and EU government\; le
 ading AI companies (Anthropic\, DeepMind\, OpenAI)\; top think tanks (CSET
 \, RAND)\; and leading universities (Oxford\, Cambridge). AMOUNT: £12\,00
 0 salary for three months\, plus travel support to London and weekday lunc
 h. Visa sponsorship for a 3-month temporary work visa is available\; succe
 ssful visa applicants must remain in their country of application while th
 e visa is processed. ELIGIBILITY: open to a wide range of backgrounds (gov
 ernment\, academia\, industry\, civil society)\; no strict degree requirem
 ents\; relevant areas include public policy\, political science\, computer
  science\, economics\, biosecurity\, cybersecurity\, China studies\, and r
 isk management. Senior candidates who need accommodations should email fel
 lowships@governance.ai with subject line 'senior candidate winter fellowsh
 ip 2027' and a CV. SELECTION CRITERIA: quality and clarity of written work
 \, relevant expertise\, judgement in prioritising research directions\, an
 d team fit (intellectual honesty\, openness to feedback\, comfort with unc
 ertainty). APPLICATION PROCESS: (1) written application and automated abst
 ract reasoning assessment\; (2) paid remote work test (August 2026)\; (3) 
 remote interview and reference checks (September 2026)\; decisions communi
 cated October 2026. Deadline: 12 July 2026\, 23:59 BST.\n\nOrganisation: C
 entre for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: London\, UK\; in-person\
 , full-time.\nAward: £12\,000 salary for the three-month term\, plus trav
 el support to London and weekday lunch provided. Visa sponsorship (3-month
  temporary work visa) available. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 months\,
  18 January to 9 April 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.governance.ai/post/winte
 r-fellowship-2027-research-track?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/post/winter-fellowship-2027-research-track?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK\; in-person\, full-time.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:govai-dc-winter-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI DC Winter Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:GovAI's DC Winter Fellowship is a three-month bipartisan in-per
 son fellowship in Washington\, DC\, designed for individuals seeking to en
 ter or advance in US AI governance and policy. The program emphasises bipa
 rtisan engagement\, rigorous analysis\, and practical policy relevance\, w
 ith a focus on how AI policy is shaped within the US political system. Fel
 lows conduct independent research on a topic of their choice with mentorsh
 ip from a GovAI supervisor\; the first two weeks explore the AI policy lan
 dscape before a research proposal is finalised. Outputs may include a repo
 rt\, white paper\, journal article\, op-ed\, or blog post targeted at an a
 udience relevant to AI policy. Fellows also attend expert Q&As\, skills wo
 rkshops\, seminars\, work-in-progress meetings\, and social events\, and a
 re encouraged to discuss follow-on career opportunities. GovAI alumni have
  gone on to roles in US government bodies (NSC\, OSTP\, Senate\, Departmen
 t of Commerce\, DHS)\; think tanks (CSIS\, CSET\, CNAS\, RAND\, Heritage F
 oundation\, FAI)\; AI companies (OpenAI\, Anthropic\, Google DeepMind)\; a
 nd universities (Yale\, Stanford\, MIT). AMOUNT: $21\,000 stipend for thre
 e months\, plus travel support to DC and weekday lunch. Part-time fellowsh
 ip at a pro-rated salary available for DC-based think tank researchers\, g
 overnment staffers\, or professionals doing AI governance work\; pay accom
 modations possible. ELIGIBILITY: candidates must have the right to work in
  the US\; no visa sponsorship. Open to a wide range of backgrounds (govern
 ment\, academia\, industry\, civil society)\; promising undergraduates wel
 come\; no strict degree requirements. Relevant areas include public policy
 \, political science\, engineering\, computer science\, economics\, biosec
 urity\, cybersecurity\, China studies\, and risk management. SELECTION CRI
 TERIA: quality and clarity of written work\, relevant expertise\, judgemen
 t\, and team fit. APPLICATION PROCESS: (1) written application and automat
 ed abstract reasoning assessment\; (2) paid remote work test (August 2026)
 \; (3) remote interview and reference checks (September 2026)\; decisions 
 communicated October 2026. Application deadline not yet publicly stated\; 
 applications open June 2026 -- check programme page for the deadline.\n\nO
 rganisation: Centre for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: Washington
 \, DC\, USA\; in-person\, full-time (part-time possible for DC-based think
  tank or government staff).\nAward: $21\,000 stipend for the three-month t
 erm\, plus travel support to DC and weekday lunch provided. No visa sponso
 rship. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 months\, 18 January to 9 April 202
 7.\n\nMore: https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/dc-winter-fellowship-2
 027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/dc-winter-fellowship-2027?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, USA\; in-person\, full-time (part-time possible 
 for DC-based think tank or government staff).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:anthropic-claude-corps-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260717
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260718
SUMMARY:Deadline: Claude Corps Fellowship
DESCRIPTION:Claude Corps is a national fellowship program from Anthropic pl
 acing early-career fellows full-time at nonprofits across America for one 
 year\, with the goal of extending AI's benefits to communities and buildin
 g fellows' AI skills. Anthropic funds the program and provides Claude expe
 rtise\; CodePath (America's largest collegiate CS education provider) is t
 he fellows' official employer of record and leads programming\; Social Fin
 ance leads measurement and evaluation. AMOUNT: $85\,000 full-time salary p
 lus benefits\; expansive Claude token budget\; relocation support as neede
 d. FELLOW EXPERIENCE: intensive Claude training at the start\; five hours 
 of ongoing weekly training throughout\; support from a CodePath mentor\; A
 nthropic office hours for technical questions\; and professional guidance 
 from the host organization manager. SAMPLE HOST ORGANIZATIONS: Braven (Chi
 cago)\, Code the Dream (Durham NC)\, Heartland Forward (Bentonville AR)\, 
 Montgomery County Food Bank (Conroe TX)\, Reef Environmental Education Fou
 ndation (Key Largo FL)\, StriveTogether (Cincinnati)\, YMCA of Greater Cha
 rlotte\, and more than 400 nonprofits total. ELIGIBILITY: anyone over 18 w
 ith under two years of full-time work experience\, regardless of education
 al background\; must be authorized to work in the US\; must be comfortable
  working with Claude and willing to relocate if necessary. COHORTS: Cohort
  1 (100 fellows\, starts October 2026) -- applications close 17 July 2026\
 ; Cohort 2 (starts January 2027) and Cohort 3 (starts August 2027) -- roll
 ing applications. Apply at https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-corps.\n\
 nOrganisation: Anthropic (funded)\; CodePath (employer of record)\; Social
  Finance (evaluation)\nLocation: USA\; in-person at host nonprofit organiz
 ations across America (placement location varies\; relocation support avai
 lable).\nAward: $85\,000 full-time annual salary plus benefits\, expansive
  Claude token budget\, and relocation support as needed. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: 12 months.\n\nMore: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-co
 rps?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-corps?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:USA\; in-person at host nonprofit organizations across America (pl
 acement location varies\; relocation support available).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wheeler-centre-next-chapter-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Wheeler Centre: The Next Chapter 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Next Chapter is The Wheeler Centre's flagship writing progr
 am\, designed to elevate Australian stories that are underrepresented and 
 to nurture a new wave of writers to tell them. Each fellow receives a AUD 
 $15\,000 stipend and is paired with a handpicked mentor for a twelve-month
  period to progress a major writing project. Fellows also receive a reside
 ncy at Varuna\, The National Writers' House\; an end-of-year publishing in
 tensive at The Wheeler Centre\; and meaningful opportunities to connect wi
 th peers\, agents\, publishers\, and readers. One fellowship place is dedi
 cated specifically to a poet: The Malcolm Robertson Fellowship. 2027 judge
 s: Melissa Lucashenko\, Micaela Sahhar\, Cher Tan\, and Melanie Saward. EL
 IGIBILITY: Australian writers. No specific genre restrictions stated\; the
  program focuses on stories that are not currently being told and voices t
 hat are underrepresented. TIMELINE: submissions open 2 June 2026\; submiss
 ions close 31 July 2026 (5pm)\; 2027 Next Chapter Fellows announced 10 Nov
 ember 2026. Questions: thenextchapter@wheelercentre.com. Apply at https://
 www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter.\n\nOrganisation: The Wheeler Centr
 e\nLocation: Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Na
 tional Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Whe
 eler Centre.\nAward: AUD $15\,000 stipend per fellow. No application fee.\
 nDuration: 12 months.\n\nMore: https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chap
 ter?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Nationa
 l Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Wheeler 
 Centre.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cht-emerging-voices-ai-society-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Center for Humane Technology: Emerging Voices in AI and S
 ociety Fellowship (Inaugural Cohort)
DESCRIPTION:CHT's inaugural Emerging Voices in AI and Society Fellowship pl
 aces 3 fellows (one per focus area) in a 6-month\, part-time program to de
 velop rigorous public-impact research and writing on how AI is reshaping s
 ociety. Fellows choose from 5 focus areas: (1) AI and Cognition — what A
 I does to human judgment\, critical thinking\, and intellectual independen
 ce\; (2) AI and Relationships — AI's effects on intimacy\, companionship
 \, and social bonds\; (3) AI and Surveillance — how AI enables monitorin
 g\, tracking\, and control\; (4) AI and Spirituality — AI's intersection
  with meaning\, transcendence\, and religious practice\; (5) Inside the Ma
 chine: AI From a Technologist's Perspective. Up to 2 focus areas per appli
 cation. DELIVERABLES: one flagship research piece\, report\, or long-form 
 essay for public impact\; 1-3 op-eds (placed in target outlets or CHT Subs
 tack)\; one podcast appearance (Your Undivided Attention or equivalent)\; 
 cohort meetings and bi-weekly check-ins. SUPPORT: bi-weekly guidance from 
 Senior Director of Strategy and Impact\; editorial\, communications and gr
 aphic design support\; media training and PR placement support\; CHT email
  address and Slack access\; listing on CHT's public fellows page. WHO SHOU
 LD APPLY: mid-career researchers (psychology\, sociology\, philosophy\, co
 gnitive science\, communications\, STS)\, policy analysts\, journalists co
 vering tech and society\, civil society advocates\, or technologists with 
 insider perspective. Strong preference for those with a public writing tra
 ck record (op-eds\, Substack\, peer-reviewed work) and some public presenc
 e. HOW TO APPLY: 1-2 page research proposal per topic (up to 2)\, CV/resum
 e (2 pages max)\, 2-minute video explaining fit\, writing sample. Deadline
 : Sunday 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EST.\n\nOrganisation: Center for Humane Te
 chnology (CHT)\nLocation: Remote\, 10 hours/week overlapping with EST requ
 ired.\nAward: $30\,000 fixed stipend paid in three milestone-based tranche
 s. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months: September 14\, 2026 through Ma
 rch 18\, 2027. 15 hours per week.\n\nMore: https://www.humanetech.com/fell
 owship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.humanetech.com/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, 10 hours/week overlapping with EST required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kb-researcher-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: KB Researcher-in-Residence (RiR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:ELIGIBILITY: two Researcher-in-Residence positions in 2027. Ope
 n to early-career researchers of any discipline (data science\, social sci
 ence\, computer science\, humanities) who are currently working on a PhD o
 r within 5 years of their PhD end date. You must have\, or be able to secu
 re\, an affiliation with a Dutch university or research institute willing 
 to second you\; external PhD students may use the funding to obtain such a
  contract. Your project must make use of one or more of the KB digital col
 lections and you must be able to work with primarily Dutch data. You must 
 be available at 0.5 FTE over 6 months and able to spend two days a week on
 -site at the KB in The Hague. COLLECTIONS: over 130 million digitized page
 s of newspapers\, magazines\, radio bulletins and books from 1470 onward (
 via Delpher and DBNL)\, a born-digital web collection of more than 23\,000
  websites\, ground-truth and derived datasets on the KB Lab\, and metadata
  including Linked Data. APPLY: use the official proposal template and subm
 it a single PDF by 27 September 2026 to dh@kb.nl\, after reading the call'
 s terms and conditions\; questions to dh@kb.nl. TIMELINE: deadline 27 Sept
 ember 2026\; internal eligibility review followed by an external committee
  review\; outcome notified November 2026\; residencies start Q1 and Q3 202
 7. DISCIPLINES: digital humanities / data science / computational research
  with cultural-heritage collections. NOTE: the output is usually a dataset
  or tool prototype hosted on the KB Lab\; you agree to share results via b
 log posts on the KB Lab and to publish resulting tools or data under an op
 en license within 6 months of the residency. Proposals should align with t
 he KB Research Agenda or the Cultural AI Lab.\n\nOrganisation: KB\, Nation
 al Library of the Netherlands\nLocation: The Hague\, Netherlands (two days
  per week on-site at the KB premises)\nAward: Secondment at 0.5 FTE for 6 
 months based on your current salary\, up to EUR 5\,590 gross per month for
  a full-time-equivalent position\, plus travel expenses\, office facilitie
 s\, and support from a research software engineer\, research advisor\, and
  collection and data specialists. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months 
 at 0.5 FTE (two days per week on-site)\; two positions in 2027\, starting 
 Q1 and Q3\n\nMore: https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir
 -2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir-2027?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Hague\, Netherlands (two days per week on-site at the KB premi
 ses)
END:VEVENT
