BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lawai-academic-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Law & AI Academic Fellowship
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for Law & AI (LawAI)\, an independent think tank 
 researching and advising on the legal challenges posed by artificial intel
 ligence\, is running its inaugural Law & AI Academic Fellowship for aspiri
 ng legal scholars who intend to go on the US legal academic job market. Th
 e fellowship is designed as an alternative to university-sponsored Visitin
 g Assistant Professor or law fellow positions: fellows spend the majority 
 of their time researching and writing articles for publication in law jour
 nals on the law of transformative AI\, and are prepared for the academic j
 ob market as part of a larger application package. Teaching is not require
 d\, which LawAI frames as a benefit over most university-affiliated positi
 ons\, though fellows who want teaching experience are encouraged to seek i
 t and LawAI and its affiliates may be able to help secure positions. Each 
 fellow is paired with an academic mentor from LawAI's network of affiliate
 d academics for ongoing support\, with more intensive help in the final st
 ages of the job application process including moot interviews and detailed
  feedback. Fellows may also inquire about spending time in residence at ac
 ademic institutions of interest. Candidates are selected in part on how we
 ll their research interests align with LawAI's: the institute expects AI t
 o reshape culture\, the economy\, public policy\, public administration\, 
 politics\, military affairs and geostrategy at least as profoundly as the 
 Industrial Revolution\, and asks whether a candidate's research is well-sc
 oped to changes of that magnitude. Areas of particular interest include ri
 sk regulation of transformative AI\, liability for AI harms\, AI and biose
 curity\, AI and cybersecurity\, executive branch use of transformative AI\
 , national security law\, law and AI infrastructure\, administrative law\,
  constitutional law\, international regulation\, AI in the judiciary\, and
  industrial policy. Full-time two-year role at USD 130\,000 per year\, bas
 ed in Washington DC\, Cambridge UK\, or remote. Applications close 31 July
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: Institute for Law & AI (LawAI)\nLocation: Washingt
 on DC\, USA\; Cambridge\, UK\; or remote\nAward: USD 130\,000 annual salar
 y. No application fee.\nDuration: Two years\, full-time\n\nMore: https://l
 aw-ai.org/career/academic-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://law-ai.org/career/academic-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA\; Cambridge\, UK\; or remote
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:rand-cast-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260801
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:Deadline: Center on AI\, Security\, and Technology (CAST) Fellowshi
 p
DESCRIPTION:RAND's Center on AI\, Security\, and Technology (CAST) runs a r
 olling fellowship for researchers working on AI security and technology po
 licy\, with mentorship from RAND policy experts. Fellows can be based in W
 ashington DC\, San Francisco or Pittsburgh\, work remotely within the Unit
 ed States\, or be based in the United Kingdom. Terms are flexible: engagem
 ents typically start at six months and can be extended up to three years\,
  full-time or part-time. US fellows receive an annual stipend ranging from
  USD 40\,000 to 200\,000 for full-time work depending on experience\, plus
  a monthly payment that can be put toward health and dental insurance\; UK
  fellows are compensated based on experience. The programme spans career s
 tages from undergraduate through mid-career. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must 
 be based in the US or UK\; US applicants need to be eligible for a governm
 ent security clearance\, and UK applicants must have a current and ongoing
  right to work in the UK. Current RAND staff are not eligible. ROLLING DEA
 DLINE: applications are submitted through an online form and reviewed quar
 terly\, with published cutoffs of 1 February\, 1 May\, 1 August and 1 Nove
 mber\, each answered by the first of the following month. The date recorde
 d here is the next cutoff (1 August 2026)\; the call does not close after 
 it. RAND notes that if one of its listed staff openings matches your inter
 est you should apply to that instead\, and suggests not applying to the fe
 llowship if you have submitted another CAST application within the last si
 x months.\n\nOrganisation: RAND Corporation\, Center on AI\, Security\, an
 d Technology (CAST)\nLocation: Washington DC\, San Francisco or Pittsburgh
 \, USA\, or remote within the United States\; fellows may also be based in
  the United Kingdom\nAward: US fellows: annual stipend from USD 40\,000 to
  200\,000 for full-time fellows depending on experience\, plus a monthly p
 ayment usable for health and dental insurance. UK fellows: compensation ba
 sed on experience. No application fee.\nDuration: Typically starts at 6 mo
 nths\, extendable up to 3 years\; full-time or part-time\n\nMore: https://
 www.rand.org/global-and-emerging-risks/centers/ai-security-and-technology/
 fellows.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.rand.org/global-and-emerging-risks/centers/ai-security-and-
 technology/fellows.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, San Francisco or Pittsburgh\, USA\, or remote with
 in the United States\; fellows may also be based in the United Kingdom
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-alignment-foundation-aiaf-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIAF Fellowship\, Autumn 2026
DESCRIPTION:An eight-week full-time empirical AI alignment research program
 me run by the AI Alignment Foundation\, with a dedicated research manager\
 , mentorship from senior researchers\, compute and infrastructure\, culmin
 ating in a Demo Day where fellows present their work. Fellows receive a US
 D 12\,000 stipend paid in two installments plus a compute budget. The prog
 ramme is fully remote for the research period\; Demo Day is hybrid in Los 
 Angeles and travel is covered for fellows who attend in person. ELIGIBILIT
 Y: open to all career stages\, including recent PhDs\, graduate students\,
  self-taught researchers and career-changers\, but candidates need a stron
 g machine learning background\, demonstrated engagement with alignment res
 earch\, prior independent research output\, and the ability to work autono
 mously. Non-US applicants are welcome provided they can make Pacific Time 
 meetings\; current graduate students may apply if they can confirm availab
 ility. This is explicitly a technical programme: the foundation says polic
 y-focused applicants should not apply. SELECTION: all applications are rev
 iewed\, qualified candidates advance to a technical test\, and finalists a
 re interviewed\, with decisions expected by early September. Applications 
 close 17 August 2026 (Anywhere on Earth)\; the programme starts 8 Septembe
 r 2026.\n\nOrganisation: AI Alignment Foundation\nLocation: Fully remote f
 or the 8-week research period\; hybrid Demo Day in Los Angeles with travel
  covered for those attending in person. Non-US applicants welcome if avail
 able for Pacific Time meetings.\nAward: USD 12\,000 stipend paid in two in
 stallments\, plus a compute budget. Travel covered for in-person Demo Day 
 attendance. No application fee.\nDuration: 8 weeks full-time at 40 hours p
 er week\, starting 8 September 2026\n\nMore: https://www.aialignmentfounda
 tion.org/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://www.aialignmentfoundation.org/fellowship?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully remote for the 8-week research period\; hybrid Demo Day in L
 os Angeles with travel covered for those attending in person. Non-US appli
 cants welcome if available for Pacific Time meetings.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:horizon-fellowship-2027-cohort@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Horizon Fellowship\, 2027 Cohort
DESCRIPTION:The Horizon Fellowship places technologists\, scientists and re
 searchers with emerging-technology expertise into US federal agencies\, co
 ngressional offices and Washington DC think tanks to work on AI\, biotechn
 ology and emerging-technology policy. The 2027 cohort runs three tiers: ju
 nior fellows (under three years of experience\, no PhD) at a USD 78\,000 b
 ase for six months\; fellows (three or more years of relevant experience\,
  or a PhD) at USD 130\,000 for twelve months\; and a newly added senior fe
 llow tier (twelve or more years of experience with a leadership background
 ) at USD 190\,000 or more for twelve months. All tiers receive a USD 17\,0
 00 benefits stipend\, a USD 3\,000 professional development stipend and up
  to USD 3\,000 in relocation assistance\, and all are potentially renewabl
 e. The programme year begins with part-time remote training from January t
 o March 2027\, followed by matching in April and May and placement from sp
 ring or summer 2027 onward. Most offers are expected by the end of Novembe
 r 2026. Placements run up to two years in total: fellows and senior fellow
 s take a one-year term at a federal agency\, congressional office or think
  tank with the option to renew for a second one-year term\, potentially on
  a different institutional track\, while junior think tank fellows take a 
 six-month term renewable once. Horizon reports that nearly all finalists a
 cross its five cohorts to date have secured a placement at a host organiza
 tion. WHAT THE FELLOWSHIP INCLUDES: dedicated training on the policy lands
 cape and applied skills such as writing and policy analysis\; a tight-knit
  peer community\; introductions to host offices central to US emerging tec
 hnology policy\; and mentorship from senior leaders and the broader Horizo
 n community. ELIGIBILITY: no prior policy experience or specific degree is
  required\, and Horizon explicitly welcomes candidates from diverse backgr
 ounds\; fellows must reside in the Washington DC area for the placement. W
 ORK AUTHORISATION: applicants need pre-existing work authorisation that do
 es not depend on employer sponsorship. Non-US citizens on E-3\, TN or H-1B
 1 visas may qualify case by case\; F-1 OPT during the first year after gra
 duation is acceptable but STEM OPT extensions are not\; green card holders
  are eligible but may face restrictions on executive branch roles. Junior 
 fellows have no specific degree requirement and may apply while finishing 
 college\, but are limited to the think tank track. SELECTION PROCESS (Sept
 ember to November 2026): application decision\, then a paid work sample of
  up to 6 hours\, a 20-minute screening interview\, a 40-minute technical i
 nterview and a 25-minute final interview. Applications close 30 August 202
 6 at 11:59pm Anywhere on Earth.\n\nOrganisation: Horizon Institute for Pub
 lic Service\nLocation: Washington DC\, USA (residence required for placeme
 nt). Part-time remote training January to March 2027.\nAward: Base salary 
 of USD 78\,000 for junior fellows\, USD 130\,000 for fellows\, and USD 190
 \,000 or more for senior fellows\, plus a USD 17\,000 benefits stipend\, a
  USD 3\,000 professional development stipend\, and up to USD 3\,000 reloca
 tion assistance. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months for junior fellow
 s\, 12 months for fellows and senior fellows\, with potential renewal. Par
 t-time remote training January to March 2027\, matching April to May 2027\
 , placement from summer 2027.\n\nMore: https://horizonpublicservice.org/pr
 ograms/become-a-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://horizonpublicservice.org/programs/become-a-fellow/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA (residence required for placement). Part-time 
 remote training January to March 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aaas-stpf-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Science & Technology Policy Fellowships\, 2027-28 Class
DESCRIPTION:The AAAS Science & Technology Policy Fellowships place scientis
 ts and engineers inside US federal agencies\, congressional offices and th
 e judicial branch for a year of hands-on policymaking\, contributing techn
 ical knowledge and analytical skills to the federal policy process. AI\, d
 ata and computing policy placements are among the largest tracks\, and AAA
 S runs a dedicated AI legislative branch cohort that places AI experts in 
 congressional offices at GS-12 Step 10 on the DC locality scale. ELIGIBILI
 TY: US citizens holding a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in any science
  or engineering field\, or a master's degree in engineering plus three yea
 rs of post-degree professional experience\; some tracks accept a bachelor'
 s plus five years. Relevant expertise includes AI\, machine learning\, com
 puter science\, robotics and data science. Fellows must reside in DC\, Mar
 yland or northern Virginia during the fellowship year. TIMELINE: the appli
 cation portal opens 1 August 2026 and closes 1 November 2026 at 11:59pm ET
 \, with eligibility notification in late November\, interview notification
  January to February 2027\, semi-finalist interviews in March\, placement 
 offers by early May\, and the fellowship year running 1 September 2027 to 
 31 August 2028. Apply through fellowshipapp.aaas.org.\n\nOrganisation: Ame
 rican Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)\nLocation: Washing
 ton DC\, USA. Fellows are placed in US federal executive\, legislative and
  judicial branch offices and must reside in the DC area.\nAward: Stipend d
 epending on experience and placement\, roughly USD 85\,000 to 140\,000\; t
 he dedicated AI legislative branch cohort is paid at GS-12 Step 10 on the 
 DC locality scale. Includes health insurance\, a travel and professional d
 evelopment allowance\, and relocation support for some placements. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, 1 September 2027 to 31 August 2028\, re
 newable in some placements\n\nMore: https://www.aaas.org/programs/science-
 technology-policy-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://www.aaas.org/programs/science-technology-policy-fellowships?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA. Fellows are placed in US federal executive\, 
 legislative and judicial branch offices and must reside in the DC area.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kennan-institute-digital-fellowship-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kennan Institute Digital Fellowship 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:The inaugural Kennan Institute Digital Fellowship promotes orig
 inal scholarship and quality non-fiction writing about Russia and the form
 er Soviet Union\, with an emphasis on work that has policy relevance. Ten 
 fellowships are awarded per year\, and AT LEAST FOUR POSITIONS ARE RESERVE
 D FOR RESEARCHERS PURSUING TOPICS IN THE NATURAL SCIENCES\, MEDICINE\, TEC
 HNOLOGY OR RELATED FIELDS\, which is the route by which a technology-focus
 ed project fits this call. The programme aims to give participants time\, 
 professional resources and support while bringing their work to a wider pu
 blic and to the Washington-based policy community\, and to build a cohort 
 network that continues into the alumni body. Each fellow receives a monthl
 y stipend of USD 2\,000\, mentorship and consultation from the Kennan comm
 unity of experts where applicable\, six virtual webinars tailored to the c
 ohort's needs\, and membership of the alumni network. ELIGIBILITY: intende
 d for individuals with several years of experience working on\, studying o
 r otherwise examining Russia and/or broader Eurasia\; applicants must have
  a good working knowledge of English\, as the fellowship is conducted only
  in English and any publications produced for the Kennan Institute appear 
 first in English with possible later translation into regional languages. 
 The programme runs entirely virtually from 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027
 \; the Institute does not cover travel-related expenses including visas. S
 upported by the Lounsbery Foundation and the U.S. Russia Foundation. Deadl
 ine 14 August 2026\, 17:00 EST. NOTE: 'digital' here describes the virtual
  format\, not the subject matter\, so this fits the desk only if your proj
 ect is itself about technology\, data or science.\n\nOrganisation: Kennan 
 Institute\, Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars (supported by
  the Lounsbery Foundation and the U.S. Russia Foundation)\nLocation: Fully
  virtual. No travel is funded and no travel is required.\nAward: USD 2\,00
 0 monthly stipend for the six-month programme\, plus mentorship from the K
 ennan community of experts\, six virtual webinars tailored to the cohort\,
  and inclusion in the Kennan alumni network of over 4\,500 people. Travel 
 and visa costs are explicitly not covered. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Six months\, 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027\, followed by several months 
 of possible integration into the Kennan Institute's event schedule\n\nMore
 : https://www.kennaninstitute.org/digital-fellowship?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.kennaninstitute.org/digital-fellowship?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully virtual. No travel is funded and no travel is required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wilson-center-research-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Wilson Center Research Fellowship\, 2027-2028 Class
DESCRIPTION:The Wilson Center\, the congressionally chartered nonpartisan p
 olicy research institution in Washington DC\, awards 10 to 20 research fel
 lowships for its 2027-2028 class. Fellows conduct research and write in th
 eir areas of interest while interacting with policymakers in Washington an
 d with other fellows. The Center accepts policy-relevant\, nonpartisan pro
 posals that address key foreign policy challenges confronting the United S
 tates and the world\, and projects must align with at least one of the Cen
 ter's four scholarship pillars: strategic competition\, economic statecraf
 t\, TECHNOLOGY AND INNOVATION\, and regional scholarship. The technology a
 nd innovation pillar is the relevant route for AI governance\, emerging te
 chnology and digital policy projects\, which the Center also supports thro
 ugh its Science and Technology Innovation Program. Fellows work remotely f
 or the duration of the fellowship\; the Center covers travel costs for two
  required trips to Washington DC at the beginning and the conclusion of th
 e term. The stipend is USD 10\,000 per month for the twelve-month term\, w
 hich runs 1 June 2027 to 30 May 2028. ELIGIBILITY: academic candidates nee
 d a PhD in hand and either a published book or monograph beyond the disser
 tation\, or a substantial body of research articles (five or more) in repu
 table peer-reviewed journals. Non-academic applicants (practitioners\, jou
 rnalists\, public intellectuals) need at least ten years of relevant exper
 ience and a record of substantial policy impact. There is no citizenship r
 estriction\, but English proficiency is required. Applications\, including
  letters of recommendation\, must be submitted online via Interfolio by 11
 :59pm ET on 30 September 2026. Questions: fellowships@wilsoncenter.org. NO
 TE: this is an open-topic foreign policy fellowship\, not an AI-specific o
 ne\; the technology angle depends on how you scope the proposal.\n\nOrgani
 sation: Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars\nLocation: Remote
  for the duration of the fellowship\, with two required trips to Washingto
 n DC (at the start and the end) for which travel costs are covered.\nAward
 : USD 10\,000 per month for a twelve-month fellowship\, plus covered trave
 l for the two required trips to Washington DC. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: Twelve months\, 1 June 2027 to 30 May 2028\n\nMore: https://www.wilson
 center.org/research-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.wilsoncenter.org/research-fellowship?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote for the duration of the fellowship\, with two required trip
 s to Washington DC (at the start and the end) for which travel costs are c
 overed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:helpmann-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Helpmann Fellowships 2026
DESCRIPTION:Three Helpmann Fellowships of up to AUD 7\,500 support ambitiou
 s\, self-directed projects that advance the artistic and professional deve
 lopment of emerging creatives. Fellowships are open across all eligible di
 sciplines\, to individuals and to collectives\, and funding can go to larg
 er-scale creative projects\, tailored professional development\, or a comb
 ination of both. Previous fellowships have supported the creation of new w
 ork\, exhibitions and performances\, residencies and internships\, touring
  and recording\, specialised training\, and long-term professional develop
 ment\; each fellowship is shaped by the recipient rather than a fixed prog
 ramme. ELIGIBILITY IS NARROW AND GEOGRAPHIC: applicants must be current re
 sidents of South Australia and either final-year students or graduates wit
 hin five years of a supported degree at Adelaide University\, the Universi
 ty of Adelaide\, Flinders University\, or the University of South Australi
 a. Honours students and graduates (up to five years after the final year o
 f study) are eligible where honours was undertaken directly following the 
 undergraduate degree. Masters and PhD candidates and graduates are not eli
 gible unless they qualify through their undergraduate or honours study. Fi
 nal-year students may apply provided the funded activity takes place after
  studies are completed and within the activity timeframe. Applications are
  assessed on artistic merit\, alignment with career development\, feasibil
 ity\, commitment to learning\, and level of ambition. The online applicati
 on requires a CV (max two pages)\, an official academic transcript\, a bud
 get on the Helpmann template\, a timeline\, project confirmations\, letter
 s of support from an academic and an industry referee\, and four to six im
 ages or one to two audio-visual work samples. An online information sessio
 n runs 4 August 2026 at 6pm ACST\, and the Program Manager\, Toni Wills\, 
 takes online or in-person application chats (info@helpmannacademy.com.au).
  Applications opened 15 July 2026 and close 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm ACST
 \, for activity occurring 1 January to 31 December 2027. Free to apply.\n\
 nOrganisation: Helpmann Academy\nLocation: South Australia (applicant must
  be a current South Australian resident)\; funded activity may take place 
 anywhere.\nAward: Up to AUD 7\,500 per fellowship\, three fellowships avai
 lable. Supported by the James & Diana Ramsay Foundation. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: Funded activity must occur between 1 January 2027 and 31 Dec
 ember 2027\n\nMore: https://helpmannacademy.com.au/helpmann-fellowships/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://helpmannacademy.com.au/helpmann-fellowships/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:South Australia (applicant must be a current South Australian resi
 dent)\; funded activity may take place anywhere.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ual-storytelling-research-fellows-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: UAL AKO Storytelling Institute: Storytelling Research Fel
 lows 2026 (six posts)
DESCRIPTION:The UAL AKO Storytelling Institute\, with the Power of Pop Fund
 \, is recruiting six Storytelling Research Fellows to run independent rese
 arch projects on how storytelling in popular culture shapes attitudes\, be
 haviour and public discourse. Fellows work across media including televisi
 on\, film\, gaming\, podcasts and digital culture\, are supported by the I
 nstitute's Head of Research and a peer cohort\, and produce actionable fin
 dings for creative practitioners\, funders and social-impact organisations
 \, presented at a closing public symposium. ELIGIBILITY: experienced resea
 rchers with a PhD or equivalent research expertise gained academically\, i
 ndependently or through professional practice\, with a track record of del
 ivering high-quality research and working with external partners. Practice
 -based and non-academic researchers are explicitly in scope. APPLICATION: 
 CV\, record of publications\, a research proposal of 1\,000 to 1\,200 word
 s\, and a supporting statement including at least one example of a researc
 h project you designed and delivered. Applications via the UAL jobs portal
 \; closes 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University of the Arts Londo
 n (UAL AKO Storytelling Institute)\, with the Power of Pop Fund\nLocation:
  High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)\nAward: GBP 48\,563 per a
 nnum pro rata (around GBP 19\,425 for the part-time fellowship hours)\, pl
 us UAL benefits. No application fee.\nDuration: Nine-month fellowship\, pa
 rt-time\, culminating in a public symposium\n\nMore: https://jobs.arts.ac.
 uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jobs.arts.ac.uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:transfyr-ai-fellowship-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Transfyr AI Fellowship 2026-2027 (Boston/Cambridge)
DESCRIPTION:A one-year\, full-time research fellowship for graduate student
 s\, recent PhDs and postdocs to lead a project on frontier machine learnin
 g problems grounded in how scientific work actually happens. THE PREMISE: 
 Transfyr argues science is missing an observability layer. Lab work is ful
 l of tacit decisions\, failed attempts\, operator judgment\, noisy instrum
 ents and context that never reaches the written protocol\, and the company
  is building what it calls the world's largest multimodal dataset of scien
 tific execution for models to reason over. Fellows get to work with that d
 ata. RESEARCH AGENDA\, eight named directions: multimodal reasoning (align
 ing video\, audio\, protocol text\, instrument traces\, timestamps\, gaze 
 and outcomes)\; conflicting evidence (uncertainty and calibration\, eviden
 ce attribution\, contradiction-aware reasoning)\; long-context scientific 
 memory (long-horizon state tracking\, retrieval over process histories\, f
 ailure and deviation memory)\; real-world evaluations (benchmark design\, 
 process-level metrics\, lab-grounded model assessment)\; tacit expertise (
 expert-novice comparison\, skill representation\, human-in-the-loop feedba
 ck)\; physical-world AI (embodied data pipelines\, transfer across setting
 s\, policy and assistance evaluation)\; biosecurity and bio-risk observabi
 lity (synthesis-to-execution verification\, emergent capability tracking\,
  autonomous vulnerability detection)\; and scientific execution ontology (
 wet-lab-grounded metadata design\, cross-domain causal attribution\, multi
 -scale variability mapping). WHAT FELLOWS DO: define a research question\,
  build prototypes\, analyse real multimodal scientific-execution data\, pr
 essure-test evaluation ideas\, and produce a publishable or demo-grade art
 ifact\, with direct mentorship from Transfyr's founders\, technical team a
 nd advisor network across AI\, biology\, robotics and scientific operation
 s. WHO CAN APPLY: current graduate students able to take leave (and to sel
 f-certify that they can commit full-time)\, recent PhDs and postdocs in ma
 chine learning\, computer science\, computational biology\, robotics\, hum
 an-computer interaction\, statistics or adjacent fields. Strong ML fundame
 ntals and evidence of research taste are the bar\; the call asks for comfo
 rt with ambiguity and curiosity about how science happens in physical envi
 ronments. International applicants are welcome and visa support is availab
 le for selected fellows. IP TERMS\, worth reading before applying: Transfy
 r owns the fellowship work product and supports publication subject to con
 fidentiality and IP review. This is a company fellowship\, not an academic
  one. APPLICATION: a one-page application sent to ai_fellowship@transfyr.a
 i saying what you want to work on and what resources you need\, with links
  that make your work easy to evaluate\; references are requested of finali
 sts. DEADLINE: applications are reviewed on a ROLLING basis\; apply by 15 
 August 2026 for full consideration\, decisions roll through late August 20
 26\, fellowship begins around September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Transfyr\nL
 ocation: Boston/Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, US\, full-time in person. Remo
 te considered by exception when the project and candidate make it workable
 .\nAward: $125\,000 annual compensation plus benefits\, plus access to com
 pute\, GPUs\, data and project support. Applicants are asked to specify th
 e compute\, GPU\, data or support their project would need. No application
  fee.\nDuration: Full-time for 12 months\n\nMore: https://fellowship.trans
 fyr.ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fellowship.transfyr.ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Boston/Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, US\, full-time in person. Remot
 e considered by exception when the project and candidate make it workable.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:agci-climate-environment-data-stewardship-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260819
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260820
SUMMARY:Deadline: Aspen Global Change Institute: Climate and Environment Da
 ta Stewardship Fellow
DESCRIPTION:AGCI is hiring a Climate and Environment Data Stewardship Fello
 w to coordinate and contribute to cross-organizational climate and environ
 mental data stewardship efforts\, supporting synergies and collective lear
 ning across established initiatives and networks focused on the collection
  and management of data used to inform climate action\, in ways that promo
 te long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperability and security.
  PLACEMENT AND MENTORSHIP: the fellow sits within AGCI's Engaged Research 
 Team\, reporting to AGCI's Research Director\, with day-to-day mentorship 
 from leaders at core partner organisations in the data stewardship space\,
  including the Data Foundation (Sonia Wang) and Public Environmental Data 
 Partners' co-founding member the Open Environmental Data Project (Brittany
  Janis)\, plus guidance from other leaders in the field. RESPONSIBILITIES:
  coordination of the coordinators\, using a network-of-networks approach t
 o build connectedness across emerging and existing work in the climate and
  environmental data landscape and to help the network identify and act on 
 shared priorities\; planning and coordinating network meetings\, workshops
  and other convenings\, starting with consolidating the landscape analysis
  exercises already underway\; elevating data stewardship work by developin
 g and sharing a comprehensive picture of what is needed to sustain data cr
 itical to climate action\; supporting peer learning through surveys\, focu
 s groups or scenario planning that enable cross-organisation conversations
  about possible futures\; connecting evolving data stewardship to the broa
 der climate action and investment ecosystem\; and contributing to the Clim
 ate Reserve\, including professional development programming alongside oth
 er Climate Reserve-supported fellows. REQUIREMENTS: bachelor's degree requ
 ired\, master's or doctorate preferred\; minimum 3 years managing communit
 ies of practice (working groups\, interagency groups and similar) or coord
 inating multi-organisation or network-based initiatives\, ideally related 
 to climate or environmental science or policy\; familiarity with data stew
 ardship principles (long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperabil
 ity\, security) sufficient to support conversations across technical and n
 on-technical partners\; public speaking experience\; experience working ac
 ross institutional\, sectoral and geographic contexts at the science-polic
 y interface\; strong project management\, research\, analysis and synthesi
 s skills\; and willingness to travel domestically and internationally. Dom
 ain expertise in any one data stewardship area is not required\, and knowl
 edge of the civil society data stewardship space is desirable rather than 
 expected. TERM AND PAY: full-time for one year at an anticipated USD 85\,0
 00 to 100\,000. DEADLINE: priority deadline 19 August 2026. HOW TO APPLY: 
 through the AGCI Workable posting.\n\nOrganisation: Aspen Global Change In
 stitute (AGCI)\, with Public Environmental Data Partners\nLocation: Remote
  anywhere within the continental United States. AGCI's primary office is i
 n Basalt\, Colorado\, under an 'Institute Without Walls' approach. Travel:
  up to 8 domestic trips a year and up to 1 international trip.\nAward: Ant
 icipated salary USD 85\,000 to 100\,000\, based on the general US market.\
 nDuration: Full-time for 1 year\; start as soon as possible and no later t
 han 1 October 2026\n\nMore: https://apply.workable.com/aspen-global-change
 -institute/j/8C263710C9/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://apply.workable.com/aspen-global-change-institute/j/8C263710C9/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote anywhere within the continental United States. AGCI's prima
 ry office is in Basalt\, Colorado\, under an 'Institute Without Walls' app
 roach. Travel: up to 8 domestic trips a year and up to 1 international tri
 p.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bavc-mediamaker-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship is a ten-month intensive for doc
 umentary filmmakers using bold cinematic language and innovative impact st
 rategies to take on critical issues of the moment. Each fellow receives US
 D 10\,000 in unrestricted funding paid out across the programme\, plus tra
 vel per diems\, and joins a cohort programme of workshops\, feedback sessi
 ons\, mentorship and industry access rather than a one-off cash award. ELI
 GIBILITY: directors of a first or second feature-length documentary\, base
 d primarily in the United States. The project must be non-commercial and f
 eature-length (shorts\, series and podcasts are excluded)\, primarily docu
 mentary or non-fiction including hybrid forms\, and demonstrate potential 
 for social impact. Only one applicant per filmmaking team may apply\, and 
 full-time students are not eligible. PROJECT STAGE: eligible films must ha
 ve at least begun production and may have started post-production\, but sh
 ould not reach a fine cut before the programme year ends\, since the point
  is to workshop the film in progress. APPLICATION: the first step is an In
 tent to Apply\, submitted through the Airtable form linked from the progra
 mme page\, due 30 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: BAVC Media (Bay Ar
 ea Video Coalition)\nLocation: United States\; applicants must primarily r
 eside in the U.S. and not expect to live abroad for a major part of the ye
 ar. Mostly remote\, with two in-person convenings in San Francisco (travel
  covered).\nAward: USD 10\,000 distributed to each Fellow over the course 
 of the fellowship\, plus travel per diems. Non-cash support includes fligh
 ts and accommodation for two San Francisco convenings\, festival and confe
 rence access with travel covered\, professional mentorship and strategy se
 ssions\, five hours of one-to-one mentorship through MediaMaker Connect\, 
 and ongoing workshops. Bay Area fellows also get access to BAVC's co-worki
 ng space and facilities. No application fee.\nDuration: Ten-month fellowsh
 ip\n\nMore: https://bavc.org/programs/mediamaker/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://bavc.org/programs/mediamaker/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; applicants must primarily reside in the U.S. and n
 ot expect to live abroad for a major part of the year. Mostly remote\, wit
 h two in-person convenings in San Francisco (travel covered).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kluge-fellowships-library-of-congress-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kluge Fellowships at the Library of Congress 2027
DESCRIPTION:The John W. Kluge Center awards twelve residential fellowships 
 a year for humanistic and social science research that draws on the Librar
 y of Congress collections\, with interdisciplinary and cross-cultural proj
 ects particularly welcome. Fellows get four to eleven months in residence 
 at a stipend of USD 5\,000 per month\, work and discussion space in the Je
 fferson Building\, and access to the Library's specialist staff\, the worl
 d's largest law library\, extensive multilingual books and periodicals\, a
 nd deep special collections of manuscripts\, maps\, music\, films\, record
 ed sound\, prints and photographs\, alongside the wider scholarly communit
 y in Washington. The Center was established in 2000 through a USD 60 milli
 on endowment from John W. Kluge. ELIGIBILITY: scholars who received a term
 inal advanced degree (the highest degree available in the discipline\, usu
 ally a PhD\, JD or international equivalent) within the past seven years\,
  in the humanities\, social sciences\, or a professional field such as arc
 hitecture or law. The degree must be in hand at the time of submission\, a
 nd full-time students are therefore out of scope. Open to U.S. citizens an
 d foreign nationals\; selected foreign nationals are assisted in obtaining
  a Library-sponsored J-1 visa\, though holders of H-1B visas are not eligi
 ble. APPLICATION: through the Library's online portal\, opening 15 April 2
 026 and closing 11:59pm on 15 September 2026. Materials required are an ap
 plication form in English\, a CV (max 2 pages)\, a 500-word research abstr
 act\, a one-page statement on how the Library's collections will serve the
  work\, a project proposal with work plan (max 3 pages)\, a bibliography (
 max 3 pages)\, and two reference letters from referees who have read the p
 roposal. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: John W. Kluge Center\, Library of Congre
 ss\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas 
 Jefferson Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any n
 ationality: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange 
 visitor visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.\nAward: Stipend o
 f USD 5\,000 per month for a residency of four to eleven months\, so USD 2
 0\,000 to USD 55\,000 depending on the tenure awarded. Twelve Kluge Fellow
 ships are awarded each year. Stipends are paid monthly by electronic trans
 fer to a U.S. bank account. No application fee\, no participation fee.\nDu
 ration: Four to eleven months in residence\; fellows may take up residence
  at any point in the 18-month window after receiving the fellowship letter
 \n\nMore: https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowsh
 ips/fellowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowships/fel
 lowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas Jeffer
 son Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any nationa
 lity: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange visito
 r visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:this-is-gender-regional-curatorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260922
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260923
SUMMARY:Deadline: This is Gender Regional Curatorial Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:This is Gender\, the photography and visual culture platform ru
 n by Global 50/50\, is launching its inaugural Regional Curatorial Fellows
  Programme for early-career curators and cultural practitioners working ac
 ross photography\, visual culture and gender justice. The premise: images 
 shape how gender\, power and social realities are understood\, feeding pub
 lic debate\, cultural memory\, research\, advocacy and policy\, yet the co
 mmunities and visual practices that should inform those narratives stay un
 derrepresented in the institutions that circulate them. The programme's ar
 gument is that diversifying who makes images is not enough\, and that it a
 lso matters who selects\, contextualises\, writes about\, commissions and 
 circulates them. For the first time\, This is Gender is embedding regional
  curators directly inside its global photography competition\, which has r
 un six cycles and receives thousands of submissions a year. WHAT THE FELLO
 WSHIP INVOLVES: working inside the competition and seeing first-hand how a
  major platform discovers\, reviews and champions new work\; shaping curat
 orial decisions by bringing regional knowledge and cultural insight into t
 he selection and interpretation of work\; contributing to public-facing pr
 ojects including exhibitions\, editorial outputs and digital storytelling 
 while developing your own writing\, framing and curatorial voice\; direct 
 engagement with international judges\, curators\, artists\, editors and cu
 ltural practitioners\; and building a network across an international coho
 rt of peers. WHO SHOULD APPLY: curators\, critics\, writers\, researchers\
 , artistic directors\, photographers\, organisers and cultural practitione
 rs from global majority countries and historically underrepresented commun
 ities. Early-career practitioners and recent graduates are strongly encour
 aged\, and a formal curatorial title is not required. HOW TO APPLY: read t
 he full role description and FAQs on the fellowship page and submit the on
 line application form\; the organisers offer the application in alternativ
 e formats and are open to a conversation before applying. Applications clo
 se 22 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: This is Gender (Global 50/50)\nLoca
 tion: Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competiti
 on. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority countri
 es and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Africa
 \, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities w
 orldwide.\nAward: Stipend of up to GBP 3\,000 for the seven-month part-tim
 e fellowship. No application fee.\nDuration: Seven months from October 202
 6\, part-time and flexible\n\nMore: https://thisisgender.global5050.org/re
 gional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://thisisgender.global5050.org/regional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competi
 tion. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority count
 ries and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Afri
 ca\, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities
  worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bold-lab-bold-fellow-oxford-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: BOLD Fellow\, British Open-ended Learning and Discovery L
 ab
DESCRIPTION:The British Open-ended Learning and Discovery (BOLD) Lab is a n
 ew UKRI-funded initiative establishing an AI research laboratory led from 
 the University of Oxford\, bringing more than 80 researchers from Oxford\,
  UCL and Imperial College London under one vision. It carries an initial i
 nvestment of around GBP 8 million with the potential for a further GBP 22 
 million extending the investment over six years\, making it one of the UK'
 s largest bets on fundamental AI research. The lab's remit is ambitious\, 
 high-risk work on the fundamental science of artificial intelligence\, alo
 ngside training future leaders in the field. THE ROLE: up to 12 BOLD Fello
 ws are being recruited across the three universities. Fellows are expected
  to operate with a high level of independence and responsibility\, setting
  their own research direction\, publishing at the highest level\, and ment
 oring junior researchers and doctoral students. They also play a leading r
 ole in managing the lab and take responsibility for developing its strateg
 ic and scientific direction\, with primary responsibility for a programme 
 of independent funded research projects. The posting frames the draw as ex
 ceptional independence\, direct collaboration with world-leading colleague
 s across the three institutions\, access to substantial and rapidly growin
 g compute\, and real influence over the intellectual direction and culture
  of a major national lab. WHO SHOULD APPLY: senior researchers with an est
 ablished\, original research track record in AI or a closely related field
 \, who are a recognised authority in their area. HOW TO APPLY: submit an o
 nline application with a covering letter or supporting statement\, a CV\, 
 and details of two referees. Informal enquiries are welcomed beforehand to
  the BOLD Exec Team at boldexec-team@eng.ox.ac.uk. NOTE: this is a salarie
 d fixed-term research post rather than a grant. Only applications received
  before midday on Tuesday 15 September 2026 can be considered.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: University of Oxford\, Department of Engineering Science (BOLD Lab\,
  with UCL and Imperial College London)\nLocation: Oxford\, UK\, with posts
  also based at UCL and Imperial College London. The 12 posts are employed 
 across the three universities.\nAward: Salary on Oxford Grade 10R\, GBP 65
 \,336 to GBP 84\,663 per year\, with the posting noting that further salar
 y options are being explored. Benefits include associate membership at Reu
 ben College\, 38 days annual leave\, pension scheme\, childcare services\,
  and travel expenses for stakeholder and conference visits. Fellows also g
 et access to substantial compute: 3 million GPU hours on Isambard for the 
 first 18 months\, with the lab working towards 5\,000 H100-equivalents in 
 total across industry and government support. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Fixed-term\, 12 months\, funded by UKRI\n\nMore: https://eng.ox.ac.uk/j
 obs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://eng.ox.ac.uk/jobs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Oxford\, UK\, with posts also based at UCL and Imperial College Lo
 ndon. The 12 posts are employed across the three universities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bulletin-editorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists Editorial Fellowship 20
 26
DESCRIPTION:The Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists runs an Editorial Fellows
  Program for emerging leaders who can give its audience regular and expans
 ive coverage of the Bulletin's core issues\, the existential threats facin
 g humanity. TRACKS: two specialty tracks are open in this round\, biosecur
 ity and disruptive technology/AI. Fellows write for the Bulletin's editori
 al pages\, producing up to four published pieces over the year at USD 750 
 each\, with limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses covere
 d on approval. WHO SHOULD APPLY: this is not a student programme. Applican
 ts need significant professional experience\, an established record of pub
 lishing quality work\, and recognition as an emerging leader in their fiel
 d\; undergraduate and graduate students may apply only if they bring subst
 antial writing experience and published work. No geographic or citizenship
  restriction is stated. HOW TO APPLY: email a resume\, a cover letter (the
  call stresses that the cover letter matters)\, and up to three writing sa
 mples\, with 'editorial fellow' as the subject line\, to the address given
  on the call page. Fellows are announced in September 2026. Deadline 28 Au
 gust 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists\nLocation: R
 emote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.\nAward: USD 750
  per published piece\, for up to four pieces annually\, so up to USD 3\,00
 0. Limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses are also covere
 d. No application fee.\nDuration: Annual fellowship\, built around up to f
 our published pieces\n\nMore: https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-
 a-2026-bulletin-editorial-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-a-2026-bulletin-editorial-f
 ellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bow-seat-true-blue-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: True Blue Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bow Seat's True Blue Fellowship funds youth-led projects that u
 se the creative arts as their primary method for building awareness or dri
 ving action on ocean\, waterways and climate change issues\, including env
 ironmental justice. Twelve fellows are selected each year and receive up t
 o USD 2\,500 in project funding alongside mentorship\, capacity-building p
 rogramming\, and access to a peer community that includes the Future Blue 
 Youth Council and Bow Seat staff. ELIGIBILITY: youth worldwide aged 16 to 
 24 at the start of the fellowship period in January. Individuals\, groups 
 and youth-led organisations may apply\, with one designated project leader
  for a group. Projects must be new or emerging\, meaning less than two yea
 rs old\, and should span at least one year running January to December. Ap
 plications must be submitted in English\, though translation tools are acc
 eptable\, and applicants should be comfortable with spoken English for pee
 r collaboration. NOTE: this is a youth programme with an upper age limit o
 f 24\, and the creative-arts requirement is central rather than incidental
 \, so proposals built on other methods are out of scope. Deadline 30 Augus
 t 2026 at 11:59pm ET.\n\nOrganisation: Bow Seat Ocean Awareness Programs\n
 Location: Remote\, open to youth worldwide.\nAward: Up to USD 2\,500 in pr
 oject funding per fellow\, plus mentorship and capacity-building programmi
 ng. Twelve youth are selected annually. No application fee.\nDuration: One
  year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://bowseat.org/programs/tru
 e-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://bowseat.org/programs/true-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to youth worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:laselle-travel-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Travel Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation established this fell
 owship in recognition of the impact travel can have on an artist's career.
  Two awards of USD 5\,000 go to artists working and residing in Texas\, en
 abling each recipient to complete a research trip that furthers their arti
 stic practice. The fellowship echoes its namesake's own history: Dorothy A
 ntoinette 'Toni' LaSelle\, a pioneer of Modernism in Texas\, travelled to 
 Europe in 1927-28 after completing her MA at the University of Chicago\, e
 xploring the art of England\, Italy and France on a trip made possible by 
 a study fund awarded for work in the First Student Art Exhibition at the R
 enaissance Society in 1926. JUROR: this year's guest juror is Lana Meador\
 , Associate Curator of Modern and Contemporary Art at the San Antonio Muse
 um of Art\, who joined SAMA's curatorial department in 2015 and has organi
 sed more than a dozen exhibitions there\, including co-curating Texas Wome
 n: A New History of Abstract Art (2020) and the ongoing Gateway series of 
 commissioned site-specific murals. She has juried artist awards for the Ci
 ty of San Antonio's Department of Arts and Culture\, the International Col
 or Awards\, Texas Sculpture Group and the Utah Artist Fellowship. ELIGIBIL
 ITY: must currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year
 \; must be a practising professional with at least three solo or group exh
 ibitions in the past three years (BFA and juried exhibitions count)\; and 
 must currently hold a degree in the arts. APPLICATION MATERIALS: image thu
 mbnails of a maximum of four works with title\, date\, medium and dimensio
 ns (JPEG or JPG only\, at least 1\,200 pixels on the longest side and unde
 r 5MB each)\; an artist CV\; a reference contact list with two names\, con
 tact details and relationship to the artist\; a project proposal of 2\,000
  characters or less covering why the fellowship matters to your practice\,
  your goals for the travel\, and when the proposed travel would take place
 \; and a copy of a valid Texas driver's licence or other proof of one year
  of Texas residency. All information and communications are in English\, w
 ith translation assistance available on request. Applications opened 3 Aug
 ust 2026\; awardees are notified 2 November 2026. Questions: info@lasellef
 oundation.org. Deadline 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm CDT.\n\nOrganisation: Do
 rothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation\nLocation: Texas\, USA. Applicants mus
 t currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year. The fu
 nded research trip itself may be anywhere.\nAward: Two awards of USD 5\,00
 0 each\, distributed in the fall of 2026\, to fund a research trip that fu
 rthers the artist's practice. No application fee.\nDuration: Travel must b
 e completed between 1 December 2026 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://
 www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Texas\, USA. Applicants must currently reside in Texas and have li
 ved there for the past year. The funded research trip itself may be anywhe
 re.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ecf-academic-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Cultural Foundation: Academic Research Fellowshi
 p (ECF Historical Archives)
DESCRIPTION:The academic track of the ECF Fellowship Programme\, which fost
 ers new research engaging with the history of the European Cultural Founda
 tion and the broader role of culture in European integration. The ECF was 
 established in 1954 by figures including Denis de Rougemont\, Robert Schum
 an\, Hendrik Brugmans and Prince Bernhard of the Netherlands\, on the beli
 ef that Europe will not be built by treaties alone. Over seventy years it 
 has produced programmes\, institutes\, campaigns and collaborations\, incl
 uding a contribution to shaping and implementing the ERASMUS student mobil
 ity scheme. That legacy is now preserved at the Historical Archives of the
  European Union in Florence alongside the records of the EU's main institu
 tions\, a placement that itself recognises the Foundation's role in Europe
 an integration. ELIGIBILITY for the Academic Research Fellowship: research
 ers in European studies\, cultural studies\, history and related fields ac
 ross the humanities and social sciences\, specifically PhD and postdoctora
 l researchers\, early-career researchers within ten years of completing a 
 master's or doctoral degree\, and independent scholars with an active rese
 arch profile. Fellows work at the HAEU embedded in the international schol
 arly community of the European University Institute\, with the archives ho
 used at Villa Salviati. Applications and all supporting documentation must
  be submitted in English through the online form. NOTE: this is the EUR 5\
 ,000 academic track. The EUR 10\,000 Artistic Research Fellowship\, open t
 o professional creatives in visual arts\, performing arts\, film and audio
 visual practices\, podcasting and audio storytelling\, documentary\, journ
 alism and literary practices\, is listed separately on the desk and shares
  the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural Foundation\, hosted
  at the Historical Archives of the European Union\nLocation: Historical Ar
 chives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Florence\, Italy\, within 
 the European University Institute.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 grant to support a r
 esearch stay at the HAEU\, covering travel\, accommodation and living expe
 nses. The parallel Artistic Research Fellowship carries EUR 10\,000 and ad
 ditionally covers artistic production. No application fee.\nDuration: Rese
 arch stay at the HAEU. Outcomes must be completed and publicly presented o
 r published within two years of signing the Grant Agreement. Deadline 30 S
 eptember 2026 at 17:00 CET.\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-ca
 lls/new-fellowship-programme-to-research-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/new-fellowship-programme-to-re
 search-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Historical Archives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Flore
 nce\, Italy\, within the European University Institute.
END:VEVENT
